[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Here are 15 CapCut [music] editing tricks that are so simple to do, but will change the way that you edit forever. We\'re going beyond just importing footage or doing auto captions in CapCut, but doing things that will change the way that your videos [music] look and feel using tricks that pro'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'CapCut editors use all the time. If you don\'t believe me, I use these CapCut tricks in almost every one of my videos and the results show. This first trick is updated CapCut filters and this allows us to achieve cinematic looking'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'footage [music] in a mere matter of seconds. We all love looking at good footage. Nobody wants something that looks terrible. This allows us to achieve that. [music] To access filters in our sliding bar at the top, click on filters and search for one called green'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yellow. Let\'s go ahead and drag this first result directly onto our footage. Now, this footage looks a lot more cinematic ready, but the real [music] trick and the hack comes from using two filters in combination. I\'m going to go ahead and'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'search for Hollywood and drag Hollywood 2 directly over my footage. By staying selected on Hollywood 2, I\'m going to decrease the value a little bit, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; click on green yellow and decrease the value some more. By selecting both of my'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'filters and clicking V, I get a before and after preview of what this footage now looks like. With two [music] filters and some quick tweaking in a matter of seconds, we have footage that looks like it came out of a movie. Another great'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'combination is by searching cyan we\'re going to drag cyan red onto our timeline and then search for dream bubble. Go ahead and drag that first result over your footage. I can see that the dream'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bubble is way too intense for my footage, so I\'m going to bring my intensity down a bit and let\'s go click on cyan red and bring that intensity down a bit as well. If I select both of them and click V, this is the before'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and this is the afterwards. For this last combination, I\'m going to search Deadpool, drag Deadpool onto my timeline, and then search for witchy wood. Drag witchy wood onto my timeline. Once again, this looks a little too dark, so'

[01:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m going to go ahead and turn witchy wood down a little bit. Now, we can turn Deadpool down a bit. Selecting both of them, this is the before and this is the after. Let\'s go ahead and select all of them, click copy, and'

[02:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'paste them over some additional footage. I\'m going to change my ratio to vertical so we have this in full screen. If I go ahead and scrub over this clip, you\'ll see that the before and the after for each of these looks so different. The three filter combinations that I just'

[02:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shared with you are only three out of so many that you can use [music] to get cinematic looking footage. If you want a full list of over 10 filter combinations, check out CapCut Lab. It\'s my beginner-friendly structured editing program designed [music] to turn anyone'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into a crazy good CapCut editor. It teaches the exact fundamentals that I use to grow my channel to over 200,000 subscribers and turn this into a full-time career. I even show you how I edited a full YouTube video and that'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video has over 200,000 views. [music] If you want to grow, learn, and take this content editing seriously, link in the description where you can find out more about CapCut Lab. This next trick is called a gradient mask and it\'s a'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fantastic tool that you can use to overlay graphics and titles in a professional way. And I\'ll show you exactly how to build it out. With our talking head video on our timeline, we\'re going to go to library in the media section. We\'re then just going to find the plain black background. [music]'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Don\'t use the one with the time code, use the image. It shouldn\'t have a time code in the top right. Let\'s go ahead and drag that over our entire clip. What we\'re then going to do is go to video, mask, add mask, and let\'s add a split mask. We can then turn this to 180° and'

[03:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drag a feather just like this. I use 78 points of feathering. We\'re then going to click on that horizontal line and drag it down as such. Staying selected on your black still, we can see the'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'effect that it\'s having on our footage. What I\'m then going to do is trim the top and the tail of my video to have the portion where we want it to kick in and the portion where we want it to animate out. Staying selected on that black still, let\'s right click and say create'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compound clip. We\'re then going to go to animation and search for slide and find slide up. I\'d recommend changing this duration to around 0.8 seconds. Then go to out and find slide down.'

[03:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can also change this duration to around 0.8 seconds. Now when we play this sequence, we have this contrast black appear from the bottom and then this goes away. This now enables us to place any form of text or'

[04:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'graphic in this part of the screen and it\'s super easy to read. This next trick is how to add depth to any talking head video using an adjustment clip and masking. This is my before footage which honestly doesn\'t look too contrasty and'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looks kind of amateur and using this trick, this is what the footage looks like afterwards. Let\'s get that effect. [music] With our video on our timeline, we\'re going to swipe in our toolbar here to adjustment a custom adjustment. We can then drag that adjustment over our'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'entire video. What we\'re going to do is stay selected on that adjustment clip and go to color wheel and let\'s bring down offset by a few points. [music] What we can then do is go to mask, add mask and let\'s create a split [music]'

[04:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mask. We can then drag this to create a diagonal line and I\'m going to try and make this parallel with my shoulder. Let\'s drag it to the left side of the screen. We\'re then going to add some feathering. I think 61 points of feathering looks'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'good. We can bring this line slightly closer to our subject. We\'re then going to add another mask and drag it in the other direction and let\'s move this over to the right side of the screen. Once again, we\'re going to add some feathering.'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Lastly, let\'s create a third mask. We\'re going to drag this 180° and drag it to the bottom portion of our screen. We can also add some feathering. Simply by toggling this adjustment clip on and off, you can see that we\'ve'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'darkened the portions of our screen that honestly aren\'t that important for our viewer to look at. This then makes our subject the main point of focus in our screen. If you want to make any tweaks, go back to those color wheels and drag'

[05:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that offset down. You can even change the saturation of everything that appears in those masked areas. With a 1-minute trick, we\'ve now created footage that looks way better because it has depth and contrast. This next trick is CapCut\'s automatic'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'background removal. Now, this may seem super trivial, but it actually enables us to do some really cool things. Oftentimes when downloading images from a website, they have embedded backgrounds in them. [music] Selecting one of those images, I can go to remove'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'background and then select order removal. CapCut perfectly removes the background. Let\'s go ahead and remove the background for the rest of my images. In one click, we have assets that we can actually use in our video to create some really cool effects. This'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next [music] trick is how to add a shadow to any element or image. So effective and so easy to do. By staying select on the element you want to add a shadow to, I\'m going to hold on option on Mac or alt on Windows. [music] On this bottom layer, I\'m going to go to'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'adjust, color wheel, and bring the exposure slider on offset all the way to zero. [music] If I select this top layer and turn it off, you can see that we have this bottom layer, which is a black [music] solid. What I\'m then going to do'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is compound this clip, go to my effects, and [music] search for blur, and find the blur option with this man with the red shirt. I\'m then going to drag blur directly onto that image, and you can then change the value of the'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'blur using this slider. Let\'s go ahead and re-enable that top clip. We can then select that compound clip and drag our shadow around to any position on the screen to create a really cool drop shadow effect. Lastly, what we\'re going to do is highlight that top layer and'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that compound clip, and let\'s create another compound clip. By compounding those two clips together, this means we can change the position, if we go back to video, the scale, you can even add animations to both those'

[07:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clips, and we always have that shadow moving with our original element. Our next trick allows our scene to come alive, and it\'s using an effect called play pendulum. Pro animators use this all the time. I\'m going to go to my'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'search bar and search for play. Find play pendulum and drag it directly onto our clip. What we\'re then going to do is turn strength down. We\'re going to turn twist down to zero, sharpen down to zero, and you can impact your speed. This affects'

[07:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how fast or slow the animation happens. If I go ahead and click play, you can see that we\'ve created now a floating element as opposed to a static element on our screen. Personally, I think the strength is a little bit too'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'high, so I can click on our main video layer, go to effects, go to that edit icon, and turn my strength down to three. That looks much better. It\'s subtle, but your viewers will be able to tell the difference. So, this next trick is less of a CapCut editing trick, but'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s for everyone building a YouTube channel. If you\'re selling a course, a product, a community, you need to understand exactly where your product sales are coming from. More importantly, exactly which of your YouTube videos are'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'driving those sales. Without this crucial insight, you could just be making content that you think is driving sales, but it actually isn\'t. Take it this way. You could be posting a video, and that video generates 50,000 views, but only generates 10 sales. Or you can'

[08:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make a video that only reaches 10,000 people, but it hits your exact right audience, and it generates you 20 sales. Without actually knowing which video is working, you keep chasing the [music] views because you think those are'

[08:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generating you sales, but your content as a dream career suffers. [music] Click.to link has revolutionized the way that I track sales from my YouTube videos. [music] It gives me direct insight into each of my YouTube videos, conversion rates, and'

[08:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'useful information so that I can keep creating the videos that actually generate sales and revenue, and not just views. This is honestly been a massive game-changer for my YouTube business. A bonus is they even have a way to change'

[09:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all the sales links in all of your YouTube videos description, [music] so you don\'t have to do it manually. Such a time-saver. If you\'re interested, check out click.to link. There\'s a link in the description. This next trick is where CapCut gets crazy good. Now, we spent'

[09:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all this time making this one visual asset look and feel really nice. There\'s a way that we can copy everything that we\'ve done on this asset over to our other clips. Let me show you how. The first thing we\'re going to do is go into our compound clip, go into our dark'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'layer, and go ahead and copy that. We can then go back to our main timeline. Let\'s create a duplicate copy of this [music] clock. Because we\'ve copied that layer, that changed our offset and exposure to zero, I\'m going to go ahead and right-click,'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'paste attributes, and go ahead and click paste. If I disable that top layer, you can see that it\'s pasted that effect that we did onto our base video layer. If I go back into our compound clip and find that video layer with our blur, I can go ahead and copy those attributes'

[10:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for that clip. Let\'s go ahead and compound this clip. I\'m going to right-click and say paste attributes. You\'ll see now that that shadow is now blurred because we took the blur effect from our previous clip and pasted it onto this clip. Lastly, if I go ahead'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and compound those clips together, I can copy the effect that we used on this clip by clicking command C, and let\'s go ahead and paste it onto our new layer. Now, we have clips that both have play pendulum on them. A little trick is to'

[10:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'change the speed value on every clip that has play pendulum. Let\'s go ahead and click edit and increase the speed to 26. Now, when I play this, you\'ll see that it looks like our objects are moving independently.'

[10:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m going to zoom out of our timeline a little bit and paste this in the bottom corner just like this. Let\'s go ahead and apply the exact same workflow to our two palm leaves. By copying over those effects to all of our elements, we\'ve created a scene now that looks alive.'

[10:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this leads us onto our next trick, which is pro text animation. It\'s one thing to be able to design a scene that looks visually aesthetic, but how do we add some textual elements to communicate a story? [music] I\'m going to go over to'

[11:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'text and add text layer. Let\'s go ahead and drag this over our entire timeline. [music] I\'m going to change this to make this and use a font called SF Pro Display, and let\'s decrease our character to minus one. I\'m also going to drag this'

[11:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into the top just like this, and let\'s go ahead and create a darker color as follows. What I\'m going to do is drag our text layer underneath our brain element. This integrates our text into the scene a little bit better'

[11:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by putting it behind our main focus point. I\'m going to go ahead and create a duplicate copy of this text layer and let\'s create a staggered effect just like this. I\'m going to change this next word to animation. Change my color to more of a red.'

[11:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And let\'s go ahead and find a new font for our accent word. I\'ve used a font called Snell Roundhand and placed it in the right side just like this. I\'m then going to create another copy of my base text layer and'

[11:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let\'s change this to in seconds. And I\'m going to go ahead and drag it to the bottom just like this. And I\'m going to decrease the size and place it under animation. Now, the [music] pro text animation and this pro text effect really comes from the effect'

[12:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that we\'re about to use. So, what we\'re going to do is select on each of these text layers individually and compound them. Then go to your effects and search for [music] player. Find the player three effect and go'

[12:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ahead and drag that onto your first compound [music] clip. What you\'re then going to do is change your speed to 25. Change your glow to whatever looks good to you. Find texture and bring this all the way down to zero. And by clicking play on'

[12:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'our timeline, you\'ll see that we get this really neat slide up effect. Now, what we can do is go ahead and copy the attributes for that specific clip. Highlight our other two clips and let\'s paste the attributes.'

[12:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, when we play our timeline, you\'ll see that we get a really smooth slide up pro text animation effect. You can change the timing of this animation simply by dragging your clips closer or further apart.'

[12:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'By dragging them closer together, you\'ll see that this animation looks a lot more smooth. This next trick is how to add a choppy or slow footage effect to your clips. I\'m going to go ahead and search for FPS lag in our effects search bar. The first result should be called FPS'

[13:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lag and we can drag that effect over our entire timeline where we want this effect to take place. By clicking play, you\'ll see that the animation of our text is now super choppy. This is because we\'re at a value of 66. [music] By sliding this down to 20, if I go'

[13:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ahead and click play now, you\'ll see that we still get that choppy effect, but it\'s a lot more exaggerated. Conversely, if I turn this up to 100 and click play, you\'ll see that the animation now is super choppy. I\'m going to go ahead and turn this down to around'

[13:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '25. Because we\'ve dragged FPS lag over all of those elements [music] and all of those elements have motion, we\'ve essentially applied this choppiness to all of those different layers. A new feature that CapCut released, which is insane for pro editing, is the'

[13:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ability to add multiple timelines in one [music] CapCut project. By going to the left here, we can go ahead and click new timeline, and it creates a [music] new timeline that we can rename, pin, delete, change the settings for on this'

[13:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sliding bar. Simply by clicking timeline one, which is now our pinned timeline, we go back to that original timeline. What\'s great about this is we can then go ahead and copy specific elements or portions of our video, click command'

[14:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] C, let\'s go to timeline two, and paste that into its own timeline. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; How this could be super useful is to make any tweaks to this direct portion of our video, or in the past when we wanted to make auto captions,'

[14:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; auto captions would automatically create captions for our entire timeline. This way we can copy a portion of our video, paste it in a new timeline, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; then create auto captions for that specific portion in our video. All we then need to do is copy those captions'

[14:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'over and paste them onto our main timeline. So, essentially, it\'s a great way to create auto captions for a specific part of your video. This next trick I use in every video, and it\'s a non-negotiable for me. It\'s adding risers to key moments in our video or'

[14:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transitions. Risers can create tension, add drama, create impact, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and justify transitions. If you\'ve never heard of a riser before, it is by far my most used sound effect. Simply by going to audio'

[15:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and then sound effects, go ahead and search for riser. You can then go ahead and play CapCut\'s wide selection of different risers. A great way to use them is for transitions or any key moment within'

[15:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your video where you want to create some sort of build-up and then a sharp drop-off. One of our goals as editors is to make a viewer watch a video and go, "Wow." If we do this, we win a huge battle for them to watch more of our'

[15:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'videos. And here\'s a cool way that we can achieve this &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; through our next effect, which is 3D text. On my timeline, I have a real estate video, and I can see in this video we have different angles that we could potentially place this 3D text'

[15:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'onto. What I\'m going to do is go to text, add [music] a default text layer, and drag it over my entire timeline. Let\'s change this to Miami and add some details about this house. Miami, $2 million.'

[15:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m also going to change the character and change this back to SF Pro Display. I know that I want this text to feature in this portion of the screen, so I\'m going to leave it right there. We can create a duplicate layer where we add some more details about this house. I\'m'

[16:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to decrease the size and drag the second text layer underneath my first. Now, you can see this text kind of looks a little bit amateur. It kind of is just placed randomly on the screen. Definitely not achieving that wow that'

[16:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we want our viewer to feel. Let\'s go to effects and search for Player 3. With our effect loaded up, we\'re going to go ahead and compound those two different text layers individually, and let\'s go ahead and drag Player 3 directly onto our first layer. I\'m going to once again'

[16:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'turn my texture down to zero, but this time we\'re going to use a new feature called rotate. Simply by dragging rotate up or down, you can see that it\'s changing the perspective of our text. At about 83 points, you can see that our'

[16:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'text now looks integrated onto that angled wall of our house. Compared to our bottom text on show Sunday, this looks so much better. So, what I\'m going to do is go ahead and copy that text layer and paste the attributes onto that'

[16:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other text layer. Now, when I play the sequence, you can see that our text actually looks integrated into our scene, not just boring flat text. These next two tricks all about creating slow motion in your footage. In our first'

[17:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clip, we just have a standard regular speed shot of people walking, and honestly, it\'s pretty bland. So, what I\'m going to do is create some artificial slow motion on this clip. Selected on my clip, I can go to speed, and you can see our speed is natively'

[17:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'set to [music] one times. I\'m going to go ahead and drag this down to 0.5 times, which is half speed or 50%. But, you\'ll see an issue when you play your footage. Now, all of a sudden our clip looks even more unprofessional because'

[17:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s choppy. So, what I need to do is go to smooth slow motion and change this from frame blending to optical flow. Now, this effect does take quite a while to process, but the results speak for themselves. What I\'m going to do while'

[17:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s processing is move on to our next clip and then show you the results of both. This next clip is not just creating slow motion for an entire clip, but a specific moment that you want to create slow motion for. So, in our clip we have a goose coming to land, and I'

[18:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want my slow motion to kick in as he\'s about to touch down. [music] So, at that moment on my timeline, I\'m going to go to speed, curve, custom, and create [music] a plus icon, which then creates a keyframe on our speed curve. Staying'

[18:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'selected on that speed curve, I\'m going to go ahead and drag this down [music] as follows. We can then slide along and select the moment that we want to return to regular speed. I\'m going to go ahead and create a plus and drag [music] the'

[18:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'second keyframe down to the portion where my first one is. Once again, we run into the same issue where now footage looks super choppy. So, I\'m going to go to smooth slow motion, activate that, and change it to optical flow. I want my slow motion to'

[18:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'be applied a little bit more gradually, so I\'m going to click on this keyframe before we go down, and I\'m going to drag it to the left to create a bit more of a time frame between those two keyframes. When I now play my clips, you\'ll see that our first video has perfect slow'

[18:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'motion applied throughout the entire video. We transformed a video that didn\'t have any slow mo into something with slow mo. And if I go to my goose landing, you\'ll see that we have regular speed, and as we reach this landing portion, our video slows down, we see'

[19:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that landing perfectly, and then speeds up again near the end. So, these two tricks are a great way to create smooth slow motion in any CapCut video. This last trick is called reactive text and it is currently blowing up all over'

[19:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'socials. If you have Reels or TikTok, I\'m sure you\'ve seen this effect over the last few months. We\'re essentially impacting the texture and the color of our text to react to the background that it\'s overlaid on. What we need to do is have a song with lyrics or a voiceover'

[19:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that we can generate auto captions for. I\'m going to go to captions and generate some auto captions. On those auto captions, we can then change the font and I\'d recommend leaving the sizing as is because we\'re going to go to'

[19:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'animation, go to captions, and I\'ve saved some that allow us to get [music] this reactive text effect. Single line, negative grow, multi-line, and multi-line three. You can select any of these just by going to all and going to'

[19:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your search bar, but let\'s go ahead and see what single line does. You\'ll see if I zoom in here that every time our background changes color or has a new texture element, that word reacts to the background.'

[20:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let\'s go ahead and select multi-line, which is going to layer some of this text on itself. We can then go back to text. Let\'s increase the size. I\'m going to place our text layer directly in the middle of the screen and let\'s go ahead and turn down line'

[20:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to minus 10%. Now, when I play this, &gt;&gt; [music and singing] &gt;&gt; pulling back the curtain by myself. reactive text effect and it\'s easy to'

[20:32] [scene:talk]
